  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: aef08fe7bbe81412eaacd58c23b63316, type: 3}
  m_Name: counted_stars
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::Data.ScriptableObjects.StoryBookDefinition
  Id: counted_stars
  Title: The Owl Who Counted Stars
  Author: Hoot
  Color: {r: 0.45, g: 0.45, b: 0.75, a: 1}
  Hint: Up on Starfall Ridge, where the stars hang low
  Pages:
  - 'My great-great-grandmother was an owl named Wren, and she was the most precise
    owl who ever lived.


    She counted everything. The acorns on her oak (two
    thousand and six). The raindrops on her branch in a shower (too many, but she
    tried). The number of times her husband said "Hm" in an evening (forty, on average;
    more in winter).'
  - 'One clear autumn night, Wren looked up and realised that nobody, in the whole
    history of owls, had ever counted the stars.


    "Well," she said. "That won''t
    do."


    She packed a satchel of biscuits, flew to Starfall Ridge, where the
    stars hang low enough to touch, settled on the highest rock, and began.'
  - 'One. Two. Three. She went from left to right, the way you read, and from top
    to bottom, the way you fall asleep.


    By midnight she had reached four thousand
    and twelve, and she was feeling very pleased with herself.


    Then a star
    sprite drifted right past her beak, giggling, and Wren lost her place entirely.'
  - '"Do you mind?" said Wren. "I''m counting."


    "Counting what?" asked the
    sprite, whose name was Lumen.


    "Stars. You''ve made me lose my place."


    Lumen
    looked up at the sky, then back at Wren, and twinkled thoughtfully. "Did I count
    as one?"


    Wren had to admit she wasn''t sure. She sighed, and started again
    from one.'
  - 'Night after night the sprites danced by, and night after night Wren lost count.


    She
    tried counting faster. She tried counting with her eyes half shut. She scratched
    tallies on the rocks with her talons, but the sprites thought the scratches were
    a game and rearranged them into pictures of owls.


    Wren was, for the first
    time in her life, thoroughly cross.'
  - 'Then came a night when the clouds rolled in, thick and grey, and there were
    no stars to count at all.


    Wren sat on her rock in the dark and felt oddly
    empty. She waited for the sky to clear. It didn''t.


    After a while a small
    light bobbed up and settled on the rock beside her. "No counting tonight?" said
    Lumen.'
  - '"No counting tonight," said Wren.


    So they talked instead. Lumen told her
    which stars were the oldest, which ones liked to show off, and which had fallen
    in love with comets and never been the same since. Wren told Lumen about acorns
    and raindrops, and her husband''s hms.


    It was, she realised as dawn came
    up, the best night she''d had in years.'
  - 'When the clouds cleared, Wren went back to the ridge. But she didn''t count.


    Instead,
    she asked Lumen the names of things. She learned the stars by heart, the way
    you learn your friends: not how many, but who.


    The counting had never really
    been the point, she decided. The sky was. And the company.'
  - 'Wren lived to be very old and very content. When she was gone, the family found
    a notebook among her things, labelled in her neat, careful hand: THE STARS. A
    COMPLETE RECORD.


    On the very first page, it said:


    "Lots. All of them
    lovely."'
  - 'Nova says it is the most accurate star chart ever made, and Nova would know.


    I
    have started counting the stars too, from my perch on the sky island. Not to
    finish. Just to keep my great-great-grandmother company.


    I am on eleven.
    I keep getting distracted. They are very lovely.'
